@page "/banner"

<CatHead Title="Banners" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    A full-width notice directly under the topbar, about the whole app: a maintenance window, an
    outage, the phase a service is in, what just happened before a redirect, and the cookie choice.
    A notice about one part of a page is an <a href="/alert">alert</a> inside it; the circuit's own
    state is the <a href="/status-bar">status bar</a>'s.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Placement and tone">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.UnderTheTopbar" Title="Under the topbar" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <code>.page-banner</code> is a sibling of <code>.topbar</code> and <code>.page</code>, in
            <code>.content</code> or <code>.bare-layout</code>, so the page scrolls below it and its words
            start on the page's own column. Four slots, each optional: an icon, the words in
            <code>.page-banner-body</code>, <code>.page-banner-actions</code> holding <code>.btn--sm</code>
            buttons, and <code>.page-banner-dismiss</code>. Name it as a landmark with
            <code>role="region"</code> and an <code>aria-label</code>. Under <code>.topbar--sticky</code>, or
            <code>.layout--flow</code> on a phone, it scrolls away under the header; below 560px the
            actions move under the words.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.Tones" Title="Tones" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <code>.page-banner--info</code>, <code>--warn</code>, <code>--danger</code> and <code>--go</code>
            &mdash; with <code>--brand</code> and <code>--secret</code>, as on every toned family &mdash; tint
            the strip and its edge and colour the icon and the dismiss; the words keep the page's ink. With no
            tone it is a quiet announcement. Pick the tone by what the reader has to do: <code>--danger</code>
            when something they rely on is broken now, <code>--warn</code> when it will be or is degraded,
            <code>--go</code> when it is over, <code>--brand</code> for news, and <code>--secret</code> while
            the reader acts as somebody else.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.Titled" Title="More than one line" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            A <code>.page-banner-title</code> first in the body makes the banner a block of text, and every
            slot then stands on its first line instead of on the middle of the block. Point
            <code>aria-labelledby</code> at the title. A link in a toned banner's words takes the banner's
            ink and an underline.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatSnippet Name="Banner/Dismiss.txt" Title="Dismissing">
        <p>
            <code>.page-banner-dismiss</code> is the app's button: remove the banner and remember the choice
            per notice, so the next notice shows again. A banner the reader cannot act on and cannot dismiss
            &mdash; an outage in progress &mdash; leaves the button out.
        </p>
    </CatSnippet>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Common notices">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.Phase" Title="Service phase" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <code>.page-banner--phase</code> &mdash; a <code>.badge</code> in the lead slot and one line
            about what the phase means for the reader, with a way to tell the team. It has no tint: it
            stays for the life of the phase, and a strip that never goes away should not compete with one
            that matters today.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.AfterRedirect" Title="After a redirect" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <strong>A success banner is for an action that ended on another page</strong> &mdash; a record
            cancelled from its own page, then the reader sent back to the list. Where the reader stays on
            the page they acted on, say it there, inline or in a <a href="/toast">toast</a>. Give the
            banner <code>tabindex="-1"</code> and move focus to it after the navigation, so a screen reader
            reads it first; show it once, and not again on a reload.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Cookie consent">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.CookieConsent" Title="Asking for consent" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            A neutral <code>.page-banner</code>, first under the topbar on every page until the reader
            chooses. The actions go inside the body, as a row under the words. <strong>Accept and reject
            look the same</strong> and sit side by side; a reject that is harder to find is not a choice.
            &ldquo;View cookies&rdquo; leads to the page that lists them and holds the same choice. Without
            a choice, set only the essential cookies.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Banner.CookieConfirmation" Title="After choosing" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            The same region, its content replaced: what the reader chose, where to change it, and a button
            that hides the message. Swap the two with <code>hidden</code> on each, or render one or the
            other; keep focus on the region so the confirmation is read.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
